<%#
  JSON safe to inline inside a <script> block.

  A bare JSON.stringify(x) in an unescaped tag is NOT safe there: it leaves "<" alone,
  so a value carrying "</script>" (a URL filter such as ?q=, a refused POST body
  echoed back as a draft, a name from the base) closes the script element and
  the rest of the payload is parsed as HTML — a reflected XSS that the CSP nonce
  cannot stop, because the injected markup is not a <script> but an <img onerror>.
  U+2028 / U+2029 are also escaped: they are line terminators for the JS parser
  but not for JSON, and would break the string literal.

  Usage, from any view (unescaped-output tag around an include):
      include('../../partials/json-script', { v: value })
  `undefined` renders as null, so a missing local never yields `var X = ;`.
-%><%
  var __js = JSON.stringify(v === undefined ? null : v);
  __js = String(__js).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')
      .split(String.fromCharCode(0x2028)).join('\\u2028')
      .split(String.fromCharCode(0x2029)).join('\\u2029');
-%><%- __js -%>